WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:06.445
A model is a select statement in a .sql file.

00:00:07.395 --> 00:00:10.744
The file name becomes the name of the table or view it builds.

00:00:11.694 --> 00:00:16.406
You never write create table, or create view. dbt writes that part for you.

00:00:17.356 --> 00:00:19.877
Here is one in Rosetta DBT Studio.

00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:25.559
Open the project, then the models folder, then staging, and pick the staging orders model.

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:31.668
It is a single select over the raw orders table: four columns, renamed and cast.

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:34.439
There is no create statement, and no insert.

00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:38.376
The source call on the last line points at the raw table.

00:00:38.656 --> 00:00:40.183
Sources are the next lesson.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.628
The second idea is ref.

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:46.089
When one model needs another, you do not write the table name.

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:50.205
You write double curly braces, ref, and the model name.

00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:52.289
That does two things.

00:00:52.569 --> 00:00:56.526
It resolves to the right table in whichever environment you are running in.

00:00:57.476 --> 00:01:00.677
And it tells dbt that this model depends on that one.

00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:02.486
Let's use it.

00:01:02.766 --> 00:01:06.699
In the marts folder, create a new file for a model called fact orders.

00:01:07.965 --> 00:01:11.713
First the orders, straight from the staging orders model, through ref.

00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:16.270
Then the payments, added up per order, from the staging payments model.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.098
Finally, one row per order, with its total amount.

00:01:20.378 --> 00:01:24.017
Notice there is not a single hard-coded table name in this file.

00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:25.584
Save it.

00:01:25.864 --> 00:01:28.555
Then open the Project menu, and choose Compile.

00:01:29.505 --> 00:01:32.103
Compile turns every model into plain SQL.

00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:34.086
Nothing runs in the database.

00:01:35.036 --> 00:01:37.105
The result goes into the target folder.

00:01:37.385 --> 00:01:41.326
Open the compiled copy of fact orders, and look at what ref became.

00:01:42.276 --> 00:01:43.767
The curly braces are gone.

00:01:44.047 --> 00:01:49.799
In their place is the full name of the staging orders view: this database, this schema, this model.

00:01:50.749 --> 00:01:56.950
Point the project at another environment, and the same model compiles to that environment's table instead.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.014
Close the compiled copy, which takes you back to fact orders, and open the Lineage tab.

00:02:04.108 --> 00:02:06.176
Studio draws this graph from your refs.

00:02:06.456 --> 00:02:11.643
The two staging models feed fact orders, and the panel on the right lists what it depends on.

00:02:12.593 --> 00:02:16.904
Raise the depth to two, and the raw tables appear behind the staging models.

00:02:17.854 --> 00:02:20.753
That graph is a directed acyclic graph, a DAG.

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:23.955
Directed, because every dependency points one way.

00:02:24.235 --> 00:02:28.346
Acyclic, because nothing can depend on itself, even through other models.

00:02:29.296 --> 00:02:32.892
dbt uses the graph to run models in a safe order.

00:02:33.842 --> 00:02:36.112
It runs independent models in parallel.

00:02:37.062 --> 00:02:41.620
And it shows you lineage: where each number comes from, and what breaks if you change it.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.744
That is the pain from Lab 1, solved.

00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:46.445
You do not manage the order.

00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:49.920
You declare the dependencies, and dbt does the rest.
